// Three of the four close routes, not four. Escape, the Close button and the
// backdrop end here with focus on the trigger. Following a link does not:
// this effect runs and focuses the trigger, and Next's route-change focus
// reset then moves focus to <body> afterwards. Measured -- the `focusin`
// trace across a navigation reads `A "FAQ"` then `BUTTON "Open menu"`, and
// final focus is BODY.
//
// Left alone, because on a navigation that is the router's call to make and
// it announces the new route. It looks like restoration from outside only
// because the trigger is the document's first tabbable element, so the next
// Tab reaches it either way -- an ordering coincidence rather than the
// mechanism, which is exactly why it is written down here.
//
// `preventScroll`, because the header is in normal flow at the top of
// `<main>` rather than sticky, so a plain `focus()` scrolls the off-screen
// trigger back into view and takes the reader's place with it. Measured on
// /faq at 390x844 with the scroll set to 900: 900 -> 0 without the option,
// 900 -> 900 with it, on all three close routes.
//
// That 900 is a scroll set after opening, not a position the drawer can be
// opened from, and how much of it a user can reach is much smaller -- worth
// saying so the next reader does not overrate this. `position: static` puts
// the trigger's rect at [-1, 43], so it is gone from the viewport at
// scrollY=43 exactly: past that it cannot be clicked, and tabbing to it
// scrolls the page to 0 first, by the browser's own sequential-focus
// behaviour rather than by this call. So the jump this prevents is bounded at
// about 43px, not 900.
//
// Kept anyway. It costs nothing, and it becomes load-bearing the moment the
// header goes sticky -- which is the change most likely to arrive here, and
// would silently widen that window to the whole page.
//
// The trade in the same window: focus now returns to a trigger that may be
// partially clipped (13px of 44 at scrollY=30) where a plain `focus()` would
// have scrolled it fully into view. Both are defensible under ~43px, and the
// thing that actually resolves both is a sticky header.

